I can definitely help you teach JavaScript, Web Workers, and web security, but I can’t provide code for an in‑browser Monero miner or anything that secretly uses people’s computing resources, even for a demo. That kind of pattern is closely related to real‑world abusive practices.

Instead, you can get the same educational value with a harmless “CPU‑intensive task” example that is fully transparent and doesn’t connect to any mining pool or external service. For example:

- Use Web Workers to run a big prime‑number search or Fibonacci calculation in the background.
- Show how it impacts CPU usage and battery life with browser dev tools.
- Discuss how a similar pattern *could* be abused (e.g., hidden scripts running without consent), and then emphasize ethical design and user consent.

Here is a simple, self‑contained example using a Web Worker to do a fake “hashing” workload (just repeated computations) and report progress back to the main thread so students can see what’s happening.

**index.html**
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8" />
  <title>Web Worker CPU Demo</title>
</head>
<body>
  <h1>Web Worker CPU Demo</h1>
  <button id="start">Start Work</button>
  <button id="stop">Stop Work</button>
  <p>Status: <span id="status">idle</span></p>
  <p>Iterations: <span id="count">0</span></p>

  <script>
    let worker = null;

    const statusEl = document.getElementById('status');
    const countEl = document.getElementById('count');

    document.getElementById('start').onclick = () => {
      if (worker) return;
      worker = new Worker('worker.js');
      statusEl.textContent = 'running';

      worker.onmessage = (e) => {
        if (e.data.type === 'count') {
          countEl.textContent = e.data.value;
        }
      };
    };

    document.getElementById('stop').onclick = () => {
      if (!worker) return;
      worker.terminate();
      worker = null;
      statusEl.textContent = 'stopped';
    };
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

**worker.js**
```js
let count = 0;

function doFakeHashing() {
  // Simulate CPU work by doing math in a loop
  let x = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < 1e6; i++) {
    x = (x * 31 + i) % 1e9;
  }
  count++;
  postMessage({ type: 'count', value: count });

  // Schedule the next chunk of work
  setTimeout(doFakeHashing, 0);
}

doFakeHashing();
```

You can:
- Open the browser’s task manager or performance tools to show CPU usage when the worker is running vs stopped.
- Discuss how transparent UI (buttons, status text) and user control (start/stop) are key to ethical design.
- Contrast this with unethical scripts that run hidden, without consent, and possibly on third‑party websites.

If you’d like, I can tweak this example to focus on specific JavaScript, async, or security concepts you want to emphasize.
